GPU processes on two GPUs send words, terms and arrays to each other,
directly with MERK_GPU_PEER=1 (runtime/device/device.cpp, Direct sends
between the GPUs of a node), and through the host without it, with the same
messages in the same order (Inputs/peer-sends.m). With
MERK_CHECK_DEVICE_MEMORY the end of the program reports how many messages
went directly and how many of the senders' copies in their GPU's send pool
were not given back, which must be none: a copy comes back when its
receiver has taken it, when its receiver ends with it still queued, and
when its receiver had ended before it was sent. Arrays from
MERK_GPU_PEER_DMA_BYTES up go into the send pool and out of it by the copy
engine, and smaller ones by the wave's lanes; each run sets it, since lit
passes it through.
REQUIRES: two-gpus

RUN: merkc -O2 -fprocess-declarations --offload-arch=%offload-arch %libomp-flags %S/Inputs/peer-sends.m -o %t
Arrays of 32 KiB by the wave's lanes.
RUN: %gpu-env MERK_GPU_PEER=1 MERK_GPU_PEER_DMA_BYTES=1048576 MERK_CHECK_DEVICE_MEMORY=1 timeout -k 1 300 %t 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=CHECK,PEER --match-full-lines

The same by the copy engine.
RUN: %gpu-env MERK_GPU_PEER=1 MERK_GPU_PEER_DMA_BYTES=1 MERK_CHECK_DEVICE_MEMORY=1 timeout -k 1 300 %t 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=CHECK,PEER --match-full-lines

A send pool of 1 MiB holds 7 copies of 128 KiB at once, so the senders
wait for their copies to come back; the copy engine makes and takes them.
RUN: %gpu-env MERK_GPU_PEER=1 MERK_GPU_PEER_DMA_BYTES=65536 MERK_GPU_SEND_POOL_BYTES=1048576 MERK_CHECK_DEVICE_MEMORY=1 timeout -k 1 300 %t 16384 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=CHECK,PEER --match-full-lines

A message larger than the send pool can hold ends the program.
RUN: not %gpu-env MERK_GPU_PEER=1 MERK_GPU_SEND_POOL_BYTES=1048576 timeout -k 1 300 %t 200000 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LARGE
LARGE: Mercury runtime: a GPU process sent a message of 1600008 bytes to another GPU, more than its GPU's send pool of 1048576 bytes can hold (MERK_GPU_SEND_POOL_BYTES)

Through the host, the same.
RUN: %gpu-env MERK_GPU_PEER=0 MERK_CHECK_DEVICE_MEMORY=1 timeout -k 1 300 %t 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=CHECK,HOST --match-full-lines

CHECK:words sent: 2000
CHECK-NEXT:words in order: 2000
CHECK-NEXT:terms sent: 1000
CHECK-NEXT:terms in order: 1000
CHECK-NEXT:a source sent: 500
CHECK-NEXT:a source sent: 500
CHECK-NEXT:arrays from both GPUs in order: 1000
CHECK-NEXT:an ending source sent: 50
CHECK-NEXT:its sink took in order: 50
CHECK-NEXT:a sink took and ended: 0
CHECK-NEXT:a source sent to it: 3000
CHECK-NEXT:a sink took 5 and ended: 5
CHECK-NEXT:its source sent: 500
CHECK-NEXT:a flood sent: 3000
CHECK-NEXT:a flood taken in order: 3000
CHECK-NEXT:Mercury runtime: 0 chunks of the devices' arenas and 0 rings are allocated
CHECK-NEXT:Mercury runtime: 0 blocks of messages between GPU processes are allocated
PEER-NEXT:Mercury runtime: {{[1-9][0-9]*}} messages went directly between GPUs, and 0 of their copies were not given back
HOST-NOT:went directly
